# Example E2 — open-gap (the claim with no assembled support)
#
# Witnesses: `gap` = EMPTY COMPLETE SUPPORT (M4a decision #8). The paper STATES the claim and
# even lays out the evidence leaves, but never assembles an argument that concludes it — no
# support term concludes improves(M, accuracy, D). With zero complete support arguments for the
# queried atom, `statusC` returns `gap` on the empty-support branch alone (Grounded.hs:127,
# spec §8), independent of any holes.
#
# This is deliberately NOT the "open mandatory CQ" mechanism: a declared argument with an open
# mandatory question is an IncompleteArgument REJECTION (Check.hs), not a gap. So E2 witnesses the
# gap the only way `runUnit` produces one — a claim whose complete-support set is empty.
#
# This is E2 of the M4a coverage matrix (docs/worked-examples-plan.md §3): status = gap, no attacks.
#
# Expected verdict is at the bottom of this file (golden oracle).

artifact resnet_c01_stated at sha256:e2e2...
policy empirical-v1
use backends [nd@1]

# --- The headline claim, stated but never argued -----------------------------------------------

claim c1
  nl      = "Method M improves accuracy on distribution D"
  formal  = improves(M, accuracy, D)
  binding = { author = alice, audit-status = unreviewed }

# --- Evidence leaves are present … -------------------------------------------------------------

# The paper's data is on the page — the report of a positive effect, and the randomization /
# power attestations — but no `arg` is ever declared to bind them into a controlled_experiment
# instance concluding improves(M, accuracy, D). The evidence sits in Γ, unused by any support term.
leaf e1 : reports(exp_1, effect(M, accuracy, D, positive))
  kind       = observed
  provenance = ai-executed
  refs       = [evidence/table_1.csv#row=mean]

leaf e2 : randomized(exp_1)
  kind       = attested
  provenance = user
  refs       = [paper.pdf#sec=4.1]

leaf e3 : powered(exp_1)
  kind       = attested
  provenance = user
  refs       = [paper.pdf#sec=4.1]

# --- … but no argument concludes the claim -----------------------------------------------------

# (Intentionally no `arg` declaration. The claim's complete-support set is therefore empty.)

status c1

# ==============================================================================================
# EXPECTED VERDICT (golden oracle) — verified by test/WorkedExamplesSpec.hs
# ==============================================================================================
# Compiled AF: no arguments.
# Claim status:
#   c1  -> gap   (empty complete support: no support term concludes improves(M, accuracy, D);
#                 statusC = Gap iff null claimSupport, spec §8 / Grounded.hs)
#
# Teaching point: `gap` is the honest verdict for "asserted but not argued". It is distinct from
# `defeated` (argued but the argument lost) — the evidence exists, but the paper never built the
# bridge from evidence to claim, so there is nothing for the calculus to accept or defeat.
